2. The technical issues I would fix before applying
I inspected the public portfolio content, several live demos, and your public GitHub repositories. These are the points I would prioritize in a technical screening.
A. MuCAT: validate the benchmark before using it as your main selling point
Your portfolio claims that MuCAT reaches 98.20% test accuracy, compared with 23.74% for the mDeBERTa-v3 ablation and 3.88% for XLM-RoBERTa-base under the same training budget. 

AI Engineer & Full-Stack Systems Architect
+1




That is an unusually large gap. It is not proof that the results are wrong, but the baseline numbers are sufficiently concerning that I would verify them before using them in interviews. On a balanced six-class task, random guessing would yield about 16.7% accuracy, so 3.88% deserves particular scrutiny if this is a six-class classification benchmark.

Check the following:
- Are the dataset, label mapping, train/validation/test split, and evaluation code identical across models?
- Is the metric calculated on the same test examples for each model?
- Are padding, tokenization, model outputs, and label IDs handled correctly?
- Is the test set independent of training, model selection, and hyperparameter tuning?
- Do the confusion matrices, per-language results, and macro-F1 support the headline accuracy?
Publish a reproducible comparison table and an ablation study. Explain the task, dataset composition, split strategy, and any limitations. Do not lead with the 98.20% figure until you can defend the entire experiment.
B. ECG v7: show the difficult metrics, not just the accuracy
Your public ECG repository is one of the more promising technical assets in your portfolio. It includes training scripts, notebooks, technical reports, a model artifact, and documentation of the MIT-BIH DS1/DS2 patient-wise evaluation protocol. 

